{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact head 4e57b68135fe5489c952fae6d3dfbf07baab1ce4. The implementation is correct and every performance claim reproduces independently on this machine; the two requested changes are small and additive, not a rejection of the approach.\n\nVERIFICATION EQUIVALENCE. The tail reuses the round-1 batch reader unchanged: internal/gitstore/audit.go has no diff against 05c0ab00, so there is no second implementation to scrutinise, only wiring. deltaScan.accept now runs the same sequence scanHead runs. ReadCommit re-hashes each object over its canonical preimage and refuses a mismatch; parents, tree, message and timestamp are then read from those audited bytes; gitstore.VerifySSHSignature checks SSHSIG in process against the expected sequencer key (armor exactness with no trailing bytes or headers, SSHSIG magic, version 1, embedded key equal to the expected wire key, git namespace, empty reserved field, ssh-ed25519 only, digest recomputed over the exact signed content); loadCommit then enforces the envelope ceiling, payload tree shape, attachment names, aggregate payload ceiling, signed-tree binding, target, causal trailers, actor signature and rotation-tree emptiness through the same batch, and accept enforces dedup against both the base map and the pending additions. This is strictly stronger than main, which took parents, tree and message from unaudited rev-list metadata and shelled out to git verify-commit per record.\n\nThe trust root is the checkpoint's verified frontier. expectedParent starts at base.Verification.Head, every record must be single-parent chained to its predecessor, and finish refuses unless the last accepted commit equals the requested head. Key rotation is verified under the key current immediately before it, then advances only the scan's own copy. The batch opens lazily on first accept, so an empty tail opens none; it is closed on every exit of both scanAfter and scanListedAfter before finish runs, and a close failure returns an error rather than publishing, so cancellation and batch-close failure cannot publish partial verification or dedup state.\n\nTHE CHECKPOINT-LISTED HALF. I probed it directly rather than reasoning about it. Reordered, dropped-middle, dropped-last, duplicated and empty manifests are all refused, by the parent chain and by the head-equality guard. The decisive case: a manifest carrying correct object ids but forged Parents, Tree, Timestamp and a forged rotation trailer in Message yields the identical correct result. The manifest supplies only an object id, and even that is proved by recomputation. Manifest data cannot substitute for verification.\n\nMEASURED. Main 05c0ab00 versus this head, same fixtures, quiet machine, identical correctness digests on every pair, and snapshot_source was verified_signed_checkpoint_tail on every checkpoint sample.\n- depth 512 tail 255: 13.341-13.565 s becomes 0.382-0.389 s. Cold audit at the same depth is 0.283 s. The 40.8x inversion this request was raised about is now 1.35x.\n- PREVIEW, depth 50,000 tail 255: 17.571-17.627 s becomes 5.870-6.005 s, against a 10 s budget and a 12.906-12.947 s cold audit at the same depth. This row is SETTLED, and a checkpoint is now 2.2x cheaper than having none.\n- FIRST-PRODUCTION, depth 500,000 tail 255: 70.522-71.323 s becomes 57.779-57.968 s, against a 60 s budget. Settled at this measurement, with about 2 s of margin. This head removes roughly 13 s of tail and touches nothing on the load side; the remaining ~57.9 s is load, so that row stays load-dominated and remains the standing risk.\n- Crossover: measured on purpose-built tail-255 fixtures, since the contract only generates tail-255 samples at depths 512 and 50,000. Cold wins at 700 and 800; the checkpoint wins from 900 up (900: 402.8 vs 404.7 ms; 1000: 395.6 vs 411.8; 1300: 431.4 vs 487.6; 2000: 504.9 vs 668.6). The claimed \"between 900 and 1000\" is confirmed to the resolution the measurement supports; I put it one step earlier, between 800 and 900. Either way it is near 900 rather than the ~47,000 before.\n\nGATES at the exact head: go build ./... , go vet ./... , go test ./... -count=1 (25 packages, all ok) and go test -race -count=1 on internal/kernel, internal/gitstore and internal/service all pass.\n\nMUTATIONS. (A) Neutralising VerifySSHSignature inside deltaScan.accept turns TestReaderRejectsWrongSequencerDeltaWithoutMutatingVerifiedCache, TestFailedMultiCommitDeltaDoesNotLeakDedupAdditions and app TestAnUnverifiableChainOutranksTheInterpreterRefusal red, as it must. (B) Reverting the two audited-metadata reads to the manifest values leaves the entire suite green. Both mutations were reverted and the worktree verified clean at 4e57b681.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Only layer 2, the kernel, is touched, and only its unexported scan internals: scanAfter, scanListedAfter, deltaScan and loadCommit. Layer 1 is untouched, with no diff in internal/gitstore. No exported contract moves, so docs/reference/architecture.md needs no change in this head. The layer's stated ownership of verification of history, object shape, signatures, ordering and payload binding, and of authenticated descendant continuation, is preserved and strengthened.\n\nSECURITY. No defect found; the trust boundary moves in the right direction on both routes. One observation, pre-existing and not blocking: the tail still learns PayloadCeiling and ObjectFormat from Descriptor, which reads the genesis message through an unaudited git subprocess. A wrong object format fails closed at validObjectID, so this is hardening rather than a hole; now that a batch is open, the genesis could be read through the audited reader with the format taken from len(genesis), as scanHead already does.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The head resolves the loadCommit batch/non-batch divergence flagged non-blocking in the cold-audit round, by deleting the non-batch path outright rather than carrying both. That is the right outcome and the main reason this review is short of a rejection.\n\nCHANGES REQUESTED, two, both small.\n1. Delete gitstore.Store.ValidatePayloadTree. This head removed its last caller and it now has none anywhere, including tests. Leaving a weaker payload validator beside the audited one in the trust-critical package is a trap for whoever later wires it back in believing it equivalent: it trusts git ls-tree output and never re-hashes. 46 lines, no behavioural risk.\n2. Add a refusal test on the checkpoint-listed route. There is none in the tree today: every scanListedAfter exercise uses a well-formed tail, and mutation B shows that this head's central security property, that the tail believes the audited object and not the manifest, has no regression guard anywhere in the repository. The head's own TestDeltaAuditUsesConstantGitProcesses cannot catch it, because an honest manifest's metadata equals the audited metadata. I have written and verified such a test; take it as-is or adapt it, at /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq/.tmp/review-4e57b681/listed_tail_refusal_test.go (drop into internal/kernel). Its forged-metadata case is the one that matters, and it dies under mutation B.\n\nNON-BLOCKING. accept mixes audited.OID and commit.OID; readObject pins them equal so this is cosmetic, but using audited throughout would say what is meant. Separately, the rotation-to-current-key guard now exists in three copies (kernel.go:951, kernel.go:1112, checkpoint.go:493) with no test on any of them; pre-existing, and worth its own request.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:683665271b51ac90a37ca87e10ef295943eb40c3","head":"4e57b68135fe5489c952fae6d3dfbf07baab1ce4","verdict":"changes-requested"}}